# Measurement harness for {{SYSTEM_NAME}}

Generated by Scheduloscope {{VERSION}}. The C sources are fixed; only
`workload.h` is derived from the model, so the measurement logic cannot drift
from what you reviewed.

## Build on QNX OS 8.0

```sh
source ~/qnx800/qnxsdp-env.sh
make qnx        # x86_64, for the QEMU quick start image
make qnx-arm    # aarch64le, for a board
```

Copy `scheduloscope-harness` to the target and run it with the privileges that
real-time priorities and priority inheritance require (root, or a process with
`PROCMGR_AID_PRIORITY`):

```sh
./scheduloscope-harness -d 30000 -o report.json
```

`-d` is the measurement window in milliseconds; the default is one hyperperiod
({{DURATION}}). `-o` writes JSON with the observed minimum, mean and maximum
response time per task, plus deadline-miss and skipped-activation counts.

## Build and run on a POSIX host

```sh
make run
```

The host backend exists to prove that the harness compiles and runs. It is
**not** a real-time scheduler: Linux will not honour QNX priorities or QNX
sporadic replenishment, so host numbers say nothing about on-target latency.

## What the harness reproduces

- one thread per task, with the modelled priority and policy (SCHED_FIFO,
  SCHED_RR, SCHED_SPORADIC with budget and replenishment);
- critical sections on mutexes created with the modelled protocol
  (PTHREAD_PRIO_INHERIT, PTHREAD_PRIO_PROTECT with ceiling, or none);
- RPCs as real channels (ChannelCreate/MsgSend/MsgReceive/MsgReply), with
  `_NTO_CHF_FIXED_PRIORITY` when the model asks for it;
- `sleep` segments as blocking sleeps;
- CPU work as calibrated busy-spin, so a segment consumes CPU instead of
  yielding the processor.

## What it does not reproduce

- `yield` and `setPriority` segments (Scheduloscope reports them when the model
  uses them);
- cache effects, memory pressure, DVFS, or the cost of the rest of your system;
- RPC timeouts (the harness blocks until the server replies).

Measure the CPU budget of each segment on the real board before trusting the
analysis. A harness number above the analytical bound means the model is wrong,
and the model is the thing to fix.
